<%#
  One system, every station.

  The page had icons and screenshots and no photography, so it read as a
  brochure for software rather than for something used in a room full of people.
  These are art-directed to the brand book — dark ground, one saturated colour,
  the screen as the light source — and none of them carries a word of text,
  because the only lettering on a Vesopa page should be Vesopa's own.
%>
<section class="vs-floor" id="the-floor" aria-labelledby="vs-floor-title">
    <div class="vs-floor-head">
        <p class="vh-eyebrow">One system, every station</p>
        <h2 id="vs-floor-title" class="vs-floor-title">
            From the counter to the pass <span>to the back office.</span>
        </h2>
        <p class="vs-floor-lede">
            The same till, the same catalogue and the same numbers — wherever in the
            building the work is happening.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="vs-floor-grid">
        <figure class="vs-shot vs-shot-tall">
            <img src="/assets/brand/counter_service.jpg" width="1264" height="848"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="A card being tapped on a payment terminal beside a till at a café counter">
            <figcaption>
                <b>At the counter</b>
                <span>Ring it up, take the card, print or skip the receipt.</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="vs-shot">
            <img src="/assets/brand/kitchen_pass.jpg" width="1264" height="848"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="A chef plating a dish beneath a kitchen order screen">
            <figcaption>
                <b>On the pass</b>
                <span>Orders arrive on screen or on paper — the kitchen chooses.</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="vs-shot">
            <img src="/assets/brand/table_service.jpg" width="1264" height="848"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="A waiter taking an order on a handheld tablet in a restaurant">
            <figcaption>
                <b>On the floor</b>
                <span>Take the order at the table and it is already on the bill.</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="vs-shot vs-shot-tall">
            <img src="/assets/brand/back_office.jpg" width="1264" height="848"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="An owner reviewing sales charts on a laptop after service">
            <figcaption>
                <b>In the back office</b>
                <span>Prices, staff, stock and the day's takings, from anywhere.</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>

    <%# The claim the page makes hardest, given a picture of its own. %>
    <div class="vs-offline">
        <img class="vs-offline-img" src="/assets/brand/offline_trading.jpg"
             width="1264" height="848" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             alt="A till still lit and working in an otherwise dark, empty room">
        <div class="vs-offline-copy">
            <p class="vh-eyebrow">When the line goes down</p>
            <h3>It keeps taking money.</h3>
            <p>
                Vesopa holds the catalogue, the prices and the open bills on the terminal
                itself. Lose the broadband mid-service and the till carries on selling —
                then syncs everything the moment it is back, with nothing lost and
                nothing typed in twice.
            </p>
            <a href="/trial" class="menu_get_button">Try it free for 14 days</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

